READ(2)                        P32W Unix                         READ(2)

NAME
       read - read from a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       ssize_t read(int fd, void *buf, size_t n);

DESCRIPTION
       read reads up to n bytes from the open file fd into buf,
       starting at the file offset, and advances the offset by the
       number of bytes read. What a read returns depends on the
       file:

       A regular file or directory gives as many bytes as remain,
       up to n; 0 at or after the end. A directory reads as its
       raw 32-byte entries (see fs(5)); dirent(3) is the portable
       way. Parts of a file never written (holes) read as zeros.

       A pipe gives what is in it, up to n bytes; if it is empty,
       read waits for a writer, and returns 0 (end of file) once no
       one has the write end open.

       A terminal in the normal mode gives at most one line,
       including its newline, waiting until a whole line has been
       typed; a ^D at the start of a line gives 0 (end of file). In
       CBREAK or RAW mode it waits for at least one character and
       gives what has arrived, up to n. See ioctl(2).

       /dev/null always gives 0. /dev/port gives n reads of the
       input port. A block device (/dev/disk, /dev/ram) reads
       through the buffer cache; /dev/rdisk reads the card itself.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of bytes read, 0 at the end of the file, -1 on an
       error with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   fd is not open, or not open for reading.
       EFAULT  buf is a bad address.
       EINVAL  n is larger than 2^31-1.
       EINTR   A signal arrived before anything was read from a
               terminal or pipe.
       EIO     A disk error on a block device or /dev/rdisk.
       ENXIO   The device does not support reading (/dev/mem), or
               /dev/tty is used without a controlling terminal.
       ENODEV  The device has no read routine.

SEE ALSO
       write(2), open(2), lseek(2), pipe(2), ioctl(2), stdio(3)

BUGS
       There are no non-blocking reads: a program cannot ask
       whether a terminal or pipe has input without waiting for it.
